Working conventions (my reading of the notation, inferred from use on the page): u=(u1,u2,u3) is a displacement field. Θ = ∂1²+∂2²+∂3² is the Laplacian, written out explicitly in line 1 and on the line 'u1 − αα ∂t²(∂1²+∂2²+∂3²)u1'. Δ_ι = ∂/∂x_ι. Δu = ∂u1/∂x1+∂u2/∂x2+∂u3/∂x3 = div u, so Δ_1Δu = ∂(div u)/∂x1. ∫…∂t² denotes a double time integral. μ, y, y' are source quantities (density times some vector, or its time derivative); m is a scalar source.

(A) Top block: specifying u by its div and curl. Riemann writes Θ(div u) = λ (a crossed-out 'm' precedes λ). He also writes ∂u2/∂x3 − ∂u3/∂x2 = ∂(μy2')/∂x3 − ∂(μy3')/∂x2 =: λ1, and indicates λ2, λ3 by '=' signs. So the curl of u (up to sign convention) is prescribed as the curl of μy', and the Laplacian of the divergence is prescribed as λ. This is the classical problem of recovering a vector field from its divergence and rotation.

(B) Equation of motion, integrated twice in time. Take a medium in which ∂²u/∂t² = α² grad div u + (other terms). Integrating twice in t gives u − α²∫∫ grad div u dt² = ξ, the remaining part. Riemann considers the combination w := u − αα∫Δ_ιΔu ∂t² componentwise and imposes:
- Δ_1(u − αα∫ΔΔu ∂t²) = X1 (the first line probably names X1 as the ι-component of such a quantity);
- Σ_ι ∂/∂x_ι [Θ(u_ι − αα∫Δ_ιΔu ∂t²)] = m, i.e. div Θ w = m (scalar source, e.g. mass density);
- Θ Δ_1(u − αα∫ΔΔu ∂t²) = Δ_1(μy'), hence ΘX1 = Δ_1(μy) (prime dropped);
- Θv = m, a Poisson equation for a potential v.
In short he splits the source of the twice-integrated equation into a gradient part (governed by the scalar m through the potential v) and a part governed by μy.

(C) Decomposition. He sets u = u' + u'' (a further term u'''' is struck out), with:
- Θu1' = ∂v/∂x1, so u' is the gradient-type part driven by the potential v;
- Δ_1u1'' = X1;
- Δu' = 0 together with ∂u1''/∂x1+∂u2''/∂x2+∂u3''/∂x3 = 0, i.e. at least u'' is divergence-free.
This is a Helmholtz-type split into irrotational and solenoidal pieces. The exact role of 'Δu'=0' is ambiguous; it may record a condition on the potential part.

(D) A struck-out attempt. Riemann first writes ξ1 = ∫Δ_1… + u1''' + μy1 and crosses it out. He then writes ∂²ξ1/∂t² = ∂²u1'''/∂t² + μy1 = ∫Δ_1Δu ∂t², and Δ_1Δu = αα Δ_1Δu''' + Δ_1X with ∂²u1'''/∂t² inserted above. This tries to identify the part of the motion produced by the longitudinal term αα grad div. The line Θ(u1 − αα∫Δ_1Δu ∂t²) = … is left unfinished.

(E) Variational formulation. He writes the functional ∫Σ(∂v/∂x_ι)² dx1dx2dx3 − ∫(Θu)² dx1dx2dx3, i.e. a Dirichlet integral for v minus a square of the u-term (possibly with coupling). He then lists the variations:
- the coefficient of δv is (∂²v/∂x1²+∂²v/∂x2²+∂²v/∂x3²), the Euler–Lagrange operator of the Dirichlet integral (up to factor −2 and boundary terms);
- the coefficient of δu1 is (ΔΔu − αα∫Δ_1Δu ∂t² + …), unfinished;
- δu2 is merely listed.
So he is seeking a minimum principle (Dirichlet principle) whose Euler equations are the system above.

(F) Quadratic invariants and a uniqueness-type argument. He lists three quantities:
- Σ(∂²u1/∂t²)² (perhaps intended as kinetic energy Σ(∂u_ι/∂t)²);
- Σ(∂u1/∂x2 − ∂u2/∂x1)², the squared rotation (shear/rotational energy of an ether);
- (Σ∂u_ι/∂x_ι)², the squared dilatation.
He then forms Σ(u1−v1)² and Σ(∂²v1/∂t²)² = 0, with a struck reference to Σ(d²x1/dt²)². Finally he forms ∂(u1−v1)/∂t and div(u−v) = ∂(u1−v1)/∂x1 + ∂(u2−v2)/∂x2 + ∂(u3−v3)/∂x3. This reads like comparing a solution u with an auxiliary or second field v and showing that the quadratic energy of the difference vanishes. That is the standard energy route to uniqueness or a least-squares characterization.

Context: the page sits between a heat-equation page and Riemann's copy of the 1858–61 Paris prize question on heat conduction. The present page is not about heat. It is about the dynamics of an elastic medium (ether) with sources m and μy, close in spirit to Riemann's published fragment 'Gravitation und Licht' / 'Neue mathematische Principien der Naturphilosophie', where ether resists volume change and change of shape.
